  "query_preview": "..\nBroad upper level ridging will persist across the inland\nnorthwest through the remainder of the week. The ridge axis\noffshore today will move to the Washington coast Thursday and \nover Eastern Washington Friday. High temperatures creeping \nupward each day. Thick smoke over the area will knock a few \ndegrees off the potential highs. Highs today in the mid 80s and\nlower 90s warming to the upper 80s to mid 90s by Friday. \nMountain valleys could approach 100 by Friday. Low temperatures \nwill be a little warmer due to the thick smoke, in the mid 50s \nto mid 60s. HeatRisk increasing to moderate across most of the \narea. Some valley locations have a 30 to 50 percent chance of \nmajor HeatRisk. \n\nUpper level ridge continuing to move east Friday. Flow aloft\nbecoming zonal over the weekend into early next week. The \nbiggest impact from this pattern change will be the increase in \nwinds. Winds remain mostly light before Friday with the\nexception of increasing westerlies along the east slopes of the\nThursday night. West to southwest winds beginning to pick up \nduring the day Friday. Winds look to ease overnight before \npicking back up again Saturday. With continued warm and very \ndry conditions, this will start to renew the concern for \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions. The strongest \nwinds are most likely to remain confined to portions of central \nWashington including the east Cascade valleys and the Waterville\nPlateau. There is a 40-70% chance of wind gusts up to 30-35 mph\nin those locations in central Washington Friday and Saturday. \nElsewhere in the Basin and into Eastern Washington and portions\nof the northern panhandle, gusts in the 15-20 mph is the most \nlikely outcome. These winds will very likely not be as strong \nas what was observed on Aug 1, especially east of Grant and \nDouglas counties. High temperatures remain in the low to mid 90s\nwith lows in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Minimum relative \nhumidity will reach the 10-25% range with limited recovery \novernight.\n\nConditions Sunday into early next week look to remain under the\ninfluence of zonal flow with weak shortwaves moving through at\ntimes. This will allow for temperatures to cool down closer to \nclimatological normals. No precipitation is currently in the forecast.\nWinds will remain diurnally driven with the potential for some \nlocally breezy winds through the Cascade gaps. LH/Felton",
